{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#1123","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#1123","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#1123\",\"parent_revision\":\"87666424c3b57e592dc73033350013d39e5b9ddb300e68216fa3470d4ee9e875\",\"root_revision\":\"87666424c3b57e592dc73033350013d39e5b9ddb300e68216fa3470d4ee9e875\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: 2026-04-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n`plugins/summarizer/tests/test_file_metrics.py` line 17 calls `sys.path.insert(0, ...)` at module level. This mutates global path state for the entire test session, causing order-dependent failures when this file runs before other tests that import the same module from a different location.\\n\\nFix: add the scripts directory to `[tool.pytest.ini_options] pythonpath` in the summarizer plugin's `pyproject.toml`, or add a scoped `conftest.py` fixture. Remove the module-level `sys.path.insert`.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-03-29T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **fix sys.path mutation at module level in test_file_metrics.py** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-03-29T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Test quality audit 2026-03-29\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-03-29\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-03-30)\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n1. Run pytest across the full test suite from the repo root:\\n   ```bash\\n   cd /home/user/claude_skills\\n   uv run pytest plugins/summarizer/tests/ -v\\n   ```\\n2. To demonstrate the order-dependency risk, run with `--import-mode=importlib` and randomised order:\\n   ```bash\\n   uv run pytest plugins/summarizer/tests/ -p no:randomly -v\\n   ```\\n3. Observe that `test_file_metrics.py` line 17 executes `sys.path.insert(0, ...)` at collection time, mutating the global path before any test runs.\\n\\n### Output / Evidence\\n\\n- `sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).parent.parent / \\\"scripts\\\"))` at `plugins/summarizer/tests/test_file_metrics.py:17`\\n- `plugins/summarizer/tests/conftest.py` already loads the module correctly via `importlib.util.spec_from_file_location` (lines 22-27) and registers it as `sys.modules[\\\"file_metrics\\\"]` \u2014 making the `sys.path` mutation redundant and unsafe\\n- No `pyproject.toml` exists anywhere under `plugins/summarizer/`; the `pythonpath` pytest option cannot be set without creating one\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n7/10 \u2014 P1. The mutation is currently masked because `conftest.py` loads first and seeds `sys.modules`, but any future test ordering change or parallel execution can expose the hidden coupling. The fix is low-risk and bounded.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Blocks: clean parallel and randomised test runs in the summarizer plugin\\n- Bottleneck: silent global state mutation in the test suite that will surface as an ordering-dependent import failure when another test in a future session imports `file_metrics` from a different path\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Removes hidden global state mutation from the test session\\n- Makes import resolution deterministic regardless of test collection order\\n- Eliminates redundancy between `conftest.py` (correct importlib approach) and `test_file_metrics.py` (unsafe `sys.path` approach)\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nRunning `uv run pytest plugins/summarizer/tests/` in any order produces the same result. The `file_metrics` module is importable from `test_file_metrics.py` without mutating `sys.path`. No module-level side effects occur at collection time.\\n\\n### Desired Structure\\n\\n- `plugins/summarizer/tests/test_file_metrics.py` line 17: the `sys.path.insert` call and the bare `import file_metrics` that follows are removed\\n- `plugins/summarizer/tests/conftest.py` remains the single source of truth for loading the `file_metrics` module \u2014 it already does this correctly via `importlib` (lines 22-27)\\n- Optionally, a `plugins/summarizer/pyproject.toml` is created with `[tool.pytest.ini_options] pythonpath = [\\\"scripts\\\"]` so that plain `import file_metrics` works without either hack\\n- If the `pyproject.toml` route is chosen, the `importlib` block in `conftest.py` is also removed to leave one canonical mechanism\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. `grep -n 'sys.path.insert' plugins/summarizer/tests/test_file_metrics.py` returns no matches\\n2. `uv run pytest plugins/summarizer/tests/ -v` exits 0 with all tests passing\\n3. `uv run pytest plugins/summarizer/tests/ -p randomly --randomly-seed=12345 -v` exits 0 (order-independent)\\n4. `uv run pytest plugins/summarizer/tests/test_file_metrics.py -v` exits 0 when run in isolation (without running conftest from the same directory first)\\n5. No `import file_metrics` outside of a function or fixture scope remains at module level in `test_file_metrics.py`\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| File | `plugins/summarizer/tests/test_file_metrics.py` \u2014 line 17 is the mutation site |\\n| File | `plugins/summarizer/tests/conftest.py` \u2014 already loads module via `importlib` (lines 22-27); determines fix scope |\\n| File | `plugins/summarizer/scripts/file_metrics.py` \u2014 the module being imported |\\n| Reference | `[tool.pytest.ini_options] pythonpath` \u2014 pytest >= 7.0 built-in option for adding source roots |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None\\n- Blocks: None directly; clean test suite is a prerequisite for the `summarizer: File Metrics & Summarization MCP` feature (#258)\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nSmall \u2014 single file edit (remove 2 lines from `test_file_metrics.py`). Optional but recommended: add `pyproject.toml` (~5 lines) and remove redundant `importlib` block from `conftest.py`. Total scope is under 20 lines changed.\\n\",\"digest\":\"58c19be90da20f87325ab12447161a0bb9eacc1bfc13c26acf2593a31458e648\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABVCt9Kw\"}"}